/*
  RHYTHM OF LIGHT — TEST 07: TWO-PLAYER IMITATION GAME

  Hardware: Arduino MKR WiFi 1010, protected piezos A0/A1/A2,
  latching switch D2 -> GND, SSD1306 128x64 OLED over I2C,
  and the existing 72-LED Vespera MQTT luminaire.

  One switch, one action per stable physical flip:
    1. Player 1 starts recording a real drum rhythm.
    2. Player 1 finishes. The original rhythm is saved.
    3. Play the ORIGINAL rhythm on the real lights.
       Wait for demonstration to finish automatically.
    4. Player 2 starts imitating the same three drums.
    5. Player 2 finishes; show an actual 0..100 match score.
    6. Start a NEW round with Player 1.

  If the switch is flipped during the demonstration, it skips
  the remaining demo and moves to WAIT FOR PLAYER 2; it does
  not falsely claim that the demonstration was completed.

  The score rewards correct drum identity (50%), relative
  timing (30%) and hit strength (20%). Missing/extra hits incur
  an insertion/deletion penalty. Event-sequence alignment uses
  two short DP rows, to fit MKR WiFi 1010 RAM.

  Times are normalized to the FIRST real hit for each player,
  so waiting before starting the rhythm is not penalized.
  Recording is RAM-only: power loss erases both performances.

  This is a real input/output test, not a simulated performance.
  No buzzer output in this standalone integration test.

  Private arduino_secrets.h needed beside the sketch:
  SECRET_SSID, SECRET_PASS, SECRET_MQTTUSER, SECRET_MQTTPASS;
  for eduroam also SECRET_WIFIUSER and ideally the university-
  verified SECRET_WIFI_CA. Never commit credentials to GitHub.
*/

